You must have played it on a REALLY crappy monitor then. I used to play
HackLite (The Software Distillery's version of Hack) on a 1000 with a Phillips
CM8833 monitor and it was just fine. I preferred it to any of the other hacks
that were around at the time because it was more stable, and the color
monsters added to the game. Other versions of Hack, (also from the Software
Distillery) such as 1.0.3d were the same, using the same graphics characters.
None of them were as bad as what Nethack is now.

What Amiga Nethack 3.0j needs is an option to play in mono. Playing in 3(4)
bitplanes on a 68000 is just way too slow on an unaccelerated Amiga. On a 68020
system it's just fine, the CPU can handle all the movement. On a 68000 Amiga it
starts to look like JRcomm scrolling, it just can't move the bitplanes fast
enough. When I had the 68000 Amiga I used to dial up the college machine
to play, because at 2400 in mono it was more enjoyable than on the Amiga.
Besides that, I got to get my name first on the high score list.

